    {
      "id": "t1jp3ws",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids and bases represent complementary chemical classes characterized by distinct chemical properties and strengths.",
        "Alkalis are a specific subset of bases that dissolve in water.",
        "The pH scale is used to measure the relative strength and concentration of acidic and basic solutions.",
        "Indicators are essential chemical tools used to identify and distinguish acids from bases.",
        "A neutralization reaction takes place when an acid reacts with a base, resulting in the formation of salt and water."
      ],
      "id": "y996vc9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5k8a2w0",
      "content": "• Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "flbcsor",
      "content": "• They have a sour taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9e4h96p",
      "content": "• They turn blue litmus paper red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yf9gu2l",
      "content": "• They react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6jifknl",
      "content": "Examples of common acids:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jtggcj6",
      "content": "• Hydrochloric acid (HCl)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "iya753e",
      "content": "• Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5ob0gss",
      "content": "• Nitric acid (HNO₃)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "lnwgug5",
      "content": "• Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"
    },
    {
      "id": "9vyp42j",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hmrns97",
      "content": "• Bases are substances that produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "are75pz",
      "content": "• They taste bitter and feel slippery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7bncl54",
      "content": "• They turn red litmus paper blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r6hbf2m",
      "content": "Examples of bases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "rpytsmk",
      "content": "• Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "eu2kr3m",
      "content": "• Potassium hydroxide (KOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5q2fio3",
      "content": "• Calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)₂"
    },
    {
      "id": "3rz8u2x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2zjvn53",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water and produce hydroxide ions. All alkalis are bases but all bases are not alkalis."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3whtox",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Properties of Acids and Bases"
    },
    {
      "id": "rcaont1",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Property</strong></p>",
